1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,870
[Music playing]
[graphic of logo: USGS, science for a changing

2
00:00:03,870 --> 00:00:04,870
world]
Yes!

3
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:06,580
Yellowstone is a volcano
An unscripted interview, January 2009, 7:00

4
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:08,570
Minutes
[video of geologist standing in front of a

5
00:00:08,570 --> 00:00:13,770
large geologic map of Yellowstone while he
talks to the camera and points out features

6
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:18,460
on the map]
Yes, Yellowstone is a volcano.

7
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:21,270
My name is Jake Lowenstern and I work for
the U.S.

8
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:22,270
Geological Survey.

9
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:25,220
I'm the Scientist-in-Charge of the Yellowstone
Volcano Observatory

10
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:30,570
and that's a partnership with the USGS, the
University of Utah who does the actual

11
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:34,670
monitoring of the earthquakes and ground movement,
and the land manager Yellowstone

12
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:35,670
National Park.

13
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:41,800
[logos of three agencies in the corner]
How do we know that Yellowstone is a volcano?

14
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:45,290
We're looking here at a Geologic map of Yellowstone
National Park and the different

15
00:00:45,290 --> 00:00:50,460
colors represent different geologic units,
different rock types, different ages of rocks.

16
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:55,309
You can see that all of the pink units in
here, these represent the youngest lava flows

17
00:00:55,309 --> 00:00:59,860
mostly that erupted at the Yellowstone volcano.
[1:00 minutes] Most of these are between

18
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:02,780
about160 and 70,000 years old.

19
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:07,030
And we see that they're covering up this area
of the park.

20
00:01:07,030 --> 00:01:09,830
It turns out this is the Yellowstone caldera.

21
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:11,909
It starts around here and it moves out into
the

22
00:01:11,909 --> 00:01:17,970
area over here and it is about 50 miles long
and it formed when this green material

23
00:01:17,970 --> 00:01:18,970
erupted.

24
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:22,979
This is called the Lava Creek Tuff and the
age of this unit is found here in the

25
00:01:22,979 --> 00:01:25,580
light green and dark green, also to the south.

26
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:28,110
The age is 640,000 years.

27
00:01:28,110 --> 00:01:31,870
This was an
enormous eruption and it spread ash over much

28
00:01:31,870 --> 00:01:33,012
of the United States [graphic showing
map of US with ash spread from offshore LA

29
00:01:33,012 --> 00:01:34,012
to offshore Louisiana to nearly Illinois,
and

30
00:01:34,012 --> 00:01:36,860
into Canada] and a similar eruption to that
today would certainly be a big deal.

31
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:39,990
But since
this time, there have been about 80 different

32
00:01:39,990 --> 00:01:41,560
eruptions at Yellowstone.

33
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:42,560
Some of them are
very big.

34
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:46,870
This one is the most recent, 70,000 years
old and it's called the Pitchstone

35
00:01:46,870 --> 00:01:48,530
Plateau lava flow.

36
00:01:48,530 --> 00:01:52,820
In fact, the size of it is about the size
of Washington DC, and it's about

37
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:55,380
100 yards thick in most places.

38
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:57,070
So this is what we're looking at.

39
00:01:57,070 --> 00:02:00,381
We can see volcanic
rocks everywhere, evidence that this is a

40
00:02:00,381 --> 00:02:01,520
really unusual place.

41
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,850
[2:00 minutes] It's a
massive volcano.

42
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:09,900
What is a Supervolcano?

43
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:13,560
Some of the eruptions from Yellowstone are
truly enormous eruptions, some of the

44
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:16,100
largest ones that are know of on Earth.

45
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,410
That includes this green unit here, the Lava
Creek

46
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:23,800
Tuff, as well as the purple unit which is
the Huckleberry Ridge Tuff which is erupted

47
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:26,080
2.1
million years ago.

48
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:30,349
So these are some of the largest eruptions
that we know of on Earth and because they

49
00:02:30,349 --> 00:02:34,599
erupted so much material on the order of about
1000 cubic kilometers

50
00:02:34,599 --> 00:02:42,280
or 250 cubic miles, then they get termed as
"super eruptions," really big eruptions and

51
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:45,190
the
way that it works, this is kind of a new terminology,

52
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:51,230
and super eruptions, if you're a
volcano that has exploded and created one

53
00:02:51,230 --> 00:02:56,120
of these deposits, then you get called a
"supervolcano" and that's where the word supervolcano

54
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:57,120
comes from.

55
00:02:57,120 --> 00:03:00,210
What is a Caldera?

56
00:03:00,210 --> 00:03:06,670
[3:00 minutes] To have this magma that's beneath
the surface and when it erupts and

57
00:03:06,670 --> 00:03:11,730
comes out, there's no longer support for the
ground surface up here, and as a result, the

58
00:03:11,730 --> 00:03:12,940
ground surface just caves in.

59
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:14,660
It founders and it falls in.

60
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:16,090
[video of model caldera collapse
made with baking flour] You're left with what's

61
00:03:16,090 --> 00:03:17,310
called a caldera.

62
00:03:17,310 --> 00:03:21,940
It's a subsidence
feature, a cave in feature, that's caused

63
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:24,629
when it loses its support of the underground
magma.

64
00:03:24,629 --> 00:03:29,549
And as a result, when it first formed, you
had a hole that was on the order of 50

65
00:03:29,549 --> 00:03:38,450
miles long and maybe even 1000, or 2000, or
3000 feet deep in places.

66
00:03:38,450 --> 00:03:42,549
Why are there geysers at Yellowstone?

67
00:03:42,549 --> 00:03:45,739
We have this deep magma chamber and there's
a lot of heat.

68
00:03:45,739 --> 00:03:49,879
The magma is generally at
temperatures like 700-800 degrees centigrade

69
00:03:49,879 --> 00:03:53,340
or something like 12-1300 degrees
Fahrenheit.

70
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:54,540
There's a lot of heat.

71
00:03:54,540 --> 00:03:58,040
That heat heats up the rock and that rock
then there's

72
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:00,470
conductive cooling, there's water down there.

73
00:04:00,470 --> 00:04:04,879
[4:00 minutes] That water gets heated up
and so much of the water that's near the surface

74
00:04:04,879 --> 00:04:09,400
at Yellowstone is actually at boiling
temperature and as you go down, it gets hotter

75
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:10,489
and hotter.

76
00:04:10,489 --> 00:04:16,159
So that creates a pressurized
system of boiling aquifer, boiling groundwater

77
00:04:16,159 --> 00:04:17,159
and that becomes unstable.

78
00:04:17,159 --> 00:04:19,150
[set of
photographs of geysers] It can cause earthquakes

79
00:04:19,150 --> 00:04:22,830
and of course, it causes the thermal
features that we see everywhere at Yellowstone

80
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:27,280
and that make Yellowstone so famous--
the geysers, the fumeroles, or steam vents,

81
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:32,550
the hot springs that are so beautiful and
contain so many kinds of life and thermafile

82
00:04:32,550 --> 00:04:33,860
bacteria.

83
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:38,740
So that's what makes Yellowstone
special and it's pretty much all due to this

84
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:40,760
underground magma system.

85
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:49,410
Some other Geologic hazards in Yellowstone
So we have these massive eruptions that are

86
00:04:49,410 --> 00:04:54,490
really quite rare and then we have these lava
flows that happen more frequently but still

87
00:04:54,490 --> 00:04:57,410
not...the last one was 70,000 years ago.

88
00:04:57,410 --> 00:04:59,740
But
this would be a really big event if it happened

89
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:03,720
again within the park. [5:00 minutes] It
might cause forest fires, it might dam up

90
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:09,000
rivers, and it would cause a lot of commotion
and anxiety within Yellowstone, but it would

91
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,820
have very little long-range effect.

92
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:14,220
It
wouldn't affect people in states that are

93
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:16,860
hundreds of miles away certainly.

94
00:05:16,860 --> 00:05:19,020
We have other
big events that occur at Yellowstone-- for

95
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:20,270
example, earthquakes.

96
00:05:20,270 --> 00:05:25,030
In 1959, there was a
magnitude 7.5 that occurred out near Headkin

97
00:05:25,030 --> 00:05:26,259
Lake, [photograph of landslide;
photograph of the Tetons] caused a lot of

98
00:05:26,259 --> 00:05:31,699
damage in the park and also caused a lot of
changes to the thermal areas within Yellowstone.

99
00:05:31,699 --> 00:05:37,100
The Teton fault is on the border of the Teton
Mountains south of the Park and it

100
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:41,639
potentially could have a very big earthquake
associated with it.Beyond that, we have the

101
00:05:41,639 --> 00:05:44,430
hydrothermal explosions or steam explosions
when the

102
00:05:44,430 --> 00:05:50,800
groundwater system becomes too pressurized
and erupts during some sort of a perhaps an

103
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:53,990
earthquake or due to a change in lake level.

104
00:05:53,990 --> 00:05:59,169
And what happens is that you get the
groundwater system can cause really large

105
00:05:59,169 --> 00:06:02,050
holes in the ground, sometimes hundreds of
feet across.

106
00:06:02,050 --> 00:06:06,470
[6:00 minutes] This is a turbid lake right
here, Indian Pond and Mary Bay.

107
00:06:06,470 --> 00:06:10,830
[photo of blue pond taken from the air] All
of these were created in the last 15,000 years

108
00:06:10,830 --> 00:06:13,770
by explosions of the hydrothermal system.

109
00:06:13,770 --> 00:06:19,259
In this case no magma's erupted, and they're
fairly localized events in terms of their

110
00:06:19,259 --> 00:06:22,410
damage, but they're still definitely relevant
for

111
00:06:22,410 --> 00:06:27,639
what's going on at Yellowstone and need to
be watched.

112
00:06:27,639 --> 00:06:32,080
Almost every year, somewhere in Yellowstone,
there's a small hydrothermal explosion.

113
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:36,639
Sometimes they may only be a couple feet across,
but we find evidence for them.

114
00:06:36,639 --> 00:06:40,389
Sometimes we see a little bit of smoke coming
out or fumes coming out in a place we

115
00:06:40,389 --> 00:06:41,960
didn't expect it.

116
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:43,990
Sometimes somebody hears it.

117
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:48,200
And in lucky cases, we actually get to
witness one of these explosions and that's

118
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:51,330
happened every few years as well.

119
00:06:51,330 --> 00:06:54,180
We have no
way of predicting these explosions, especially

120
00:06:54,180 --> 00:06:58,621
the small ones, but we hope that as time
goes by, [7:00 minutes] we'll get more and

121
00:06:58,621 --> 00:07:02,210
more knowledge about how they work and
maybe get the chance that some day, we'll

122
00:07:02,210 --> 00:07:07,130
be able to have some predictive capabilities
for these explosions.

123
00:07:07,130 --> 00:07:08,919
[Music playing]
[credits]

124
00:07:08,919 --> 00:07:09,919
Yes!

125
